    *** Release 0.9.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.8.0:

    New features:
        - Draw the emulated VGA's picture with SIMD instructions, each
          processor using the fastest of SSE2, SSSE3, SSE4.1, AVX, and AVX2
          that it has, as timed at start, and share the drawing of each frame
          among the processors, above all those that wait, such as the ones
          an OS for one processor never starts. Each frame is drawn whole
          before it reaches the screen, which never shows part of one.

    Bug fixes:
        - Leave the IRQs that the HPET takes out of the other legacy
          devices' resources, where Windows XP found the real-time clock's
          IRQ claimed twice and gave it Code 12.
        - Put the legacy devices whose ports no PCI root bus window routes,
          as on the Dell Wyse 5070, under \_SB rather than the PCI bus, where
          Windows XP found their ports unavailable and gave the real-time
          clock Code 12.
        - Set up the host's registers of a PCI function again after its
          driver resets it, which takes its BARs, Resizable BAR sizes, bridge
          windows, and MSI and MSI-X setup back to their defaults.
        - Show at once what emulated instructions write to the linear
          framebuffer of the emulated VGA's VBE modes, which showed up to a
          second later.

2026-11-11  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.8.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.7.1:

    New features:
        - Smooth the emulated VGA's picture with bilinear filtering where its
          scale is no whole number, as with stretched modes. A whole-number
          scale stays pixel-exact, and vga_filter = nearest brings back the
          sharp look.
        - Report the system board's devices, and the memory that ReBIOS takes
          for itself among the PCI addresses, through the Plug and Play
          BIOS's system device nodes, for OSes that use it rather than ACPI,
          such as Windows 95.
        - Add the pci_high_windows setting, off by default, which places BARs
          too large to go below 4 GiB above it, in root bus windows there, as
          firmware's "Above 4G Decoding" option does. Without it, such BARs
          stay unassigned, and the log names them.

    Bug fixes:
        - Describe PCI windows above 4 GiB only with pci_high_windows, and
          then to every OS: 0.7.1 gave them to OSes that answer _OSI for
          Windows Vista, as Windows XP with a later Windows's ACPI driver
          does, which then stopped with STOP 0x7B.
        - Claim the emulated VGA's linear framebuffer, which no BAR holds, in
          the motherboard resources, the Plug and Play BIOS, and the memory
          map, so that no OS gives its addresses to a device.
        - Read USB keyboards whose boot keyboard interfaces have no keys,
          such as the ROG Ally's, through their report descriptors, as those
          keyboards ignore the switch to the boot format: Ctrl, Shift, Alt,
          and the Windows key looked held down.

2026-11-10  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.7.1 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.7.0:

    New features:
        - Run the Video BIOS of AMD's APUs with Vega graphics by default
          (Raven Ridge, Picasso, Renoir, Lucienne, Cezanne, and Barcelo), now
          that ReBIOS leaves the firmware's graphics driver running, without
          which their Video BIOS hung.

    Bug fixes:
        - Turn on AMD's 8-bit APIC IDs, for the machine and for the OS, where
          a processor's APIC ID reaches 15: with 4-bit IDs, 15 is the
          broadcast ID, so the INIT with which the OS starts processor 15
          reached every processor, and restarted 8-core Ryzen laptops.
        - Turn on the decoding of the IDE channels that the firmware left off
          on Intel's IDE controllers: without it, Windows XP stopped at boot
          with STOP 0x7B.
        - Leave the ports of ReBIOS's own legacy devices, such as the RTC's,
          out of the firmware's motherboard resources, whose overlap Windows
          XP showed as a conflict (Code 12).
        - Give the PCI windows above 4 GiB only to OSes that answer _OSI for
          Windows Vista, as later Windows and Linux do: Windows XP failed to
          start with one, which machines with BARs over 256 MiB had.
        - Leave room in the OS's PCI memory for what the OS places there
          itself, such as Linux's windows for bridges with hotplug slots,
          which it could not assign: 64 MiB more in each root bus's region
          below 4 GiB, and 16 GiB above 4 GiB on the OS's bus 0.
        - Keep the local APICs in the x2APIC mode that the firmware left them
          in, rather than leaving it through the disabled state, which clears
          their registers.
        - Measure the TSC's rate against the PM timer alone, and no longer
          check the PM timer's rate against the HPET or the firmware's
          Stall(), which could replace a sound PM timer with ReBIOS's own.
        - Check the VM entries and VMX instructions of the OS's own
          hypervisors as Intel's processors do: the host address-space size
          against the processor's mode, and the segments of memory operands
          outside 64-bit mode.

2026-11-09  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.7.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.6.0:

    New features:
        - Give the OS an RTC and CMOS RAM of its own: the machine's clock
          where the firmware's FADT allows it and the RTC reads sanely, else
          an emulated one set from the firmware's time, and CMOS RAM that no
          longer reaches the machine's.
        - Give the OS the PC/AT's 8237 DMA controllers, a reset control
          register at CF9h of its own, and, for OSes that clear CR0.NE, the
          PC/AT's report of x87 errors on IRQ13, with port F0h.
        - Give the OS the serial and parallel ports that the firmware's ACPI
          describes, with their interrupts, including those the OS turns on
          or moves, but for the one ReBIOS logs to. The other legacy ports
          read as absent, as on a board without those devices.
        - Describe ReBIOS's legacy devices in the OS's ACPI namespace in place
          of the firmware's: the 8259s, the 8254, the RTC, the speaker's port,
          the 8237s, the x87 error port, and the PS/2 keyboard and mouse.
        - Take the serial log's port from the firmware's SPCR or DBG2 table:
          `serial_port` defaults to `auto`.
        - For OSes without ACPI, do what the firmware does outside ACPI mode:
          power the machine off at a press of the power button, a
          control-method one too, and watch the firmware's thermal zones,
          powering the machine off past their critical and hot trip points,
          running their fans past their active ones, and slowing their
          processors down past their passive ones, until the OS's own ACPI
          takes them over.
        - Give the OS a PM timer of its own, counted from the TSC, where the
          machine's stands still, goes backwards, or runs at the wrong rate.
        - Run the machine's local APICs in x2APIC mode wherever the processor
          has it and the firmware's DMAR table does not opt out of it, with
          the IOMMU remapping interrupts to x2APIC destinations, choosing the
          mode once at boot rather than following the OS's.
        - With `save_log`, keep ReBIOS's log in EFI variables while the OS
          runs, for machines without a serial port: the next start of ReBIOS
          writes it to `rebios-last.log` beside `rebios.efi`.
        - Report the DMA faults that the IOMMUs record while the OS runs, not
          only while the legacy BIOS starts.
        - Make the emulated VGA several times faster: repeated string
          instructions on its memory, and repeated OUTS from RAM, run in bulk
          rather than an iteration at a time, its writes reach all four
          planes at once, MOVSB copies through its latches go a run at a
          time, and its redraws take a half to a fifth of the time. Every
          emulated memory access also reads less of the processor's state.
        - Load the microcode updates that the OS gives the processor, on
          Intel's and AMD's processors alike, as the OS would on bare metal,
          which a hypervisor's guest cannot do itself.
        - Show the OS the BARs it gave a device wherever the device gives its
          configuration space a second way: the copies that NVIDIA's and AMD's
          GPUs keep in their BARs and reach through their ports, and the
          RTL8168's window onto its MSI-X table.

    Bug fixes:
        - Reach the machine's legacy devices only where its firmware describes
          them, and its quirks allow: mask the 8259s only where the MADT has
          them, sound the speaker only where ACPI has the 8254 and port 61h
          and the 8254 counts, probe the 8042 only as Linux 7.3 does, pulse
          its reset line only where one exists, and use CF9h only where the
          PCI configuration ports answer, with Linux 7.3's values. Port 92h
          and the PCH's 8254 gate are left alone.
        - Run the firmware's AML and ReBIOS's own accesses to the RTC through
          one owner, so that neither changes the index register under the
          other.
        - Split wide port accesses to 8-bit devices into bytes, as an ISA bus
          does.
        - Pass the OS only the interrupt lines of the devices it gets, and
          follow its HPET's legacy replacement route, which IRQs 0 and 8
          take.
        - Raise INIT from the keyboard controller's reset line, as the
          chipset does: the legacy BIOS then resumes where the CMOS shutdown
          byte asks it to, or resets the machine.
        - Fill the OS's A20-masked view of memory as it reaches it, rather
          than all at once when the gate first closes, which kept the
          processor from the OS for tens of milliseconds.
        - Check the firmware's tables and the hardware before trusting them,
          as Linux 7.3 does: memory map entries that overlap, or that are
          specific-purpose or uncacheable, SMBIOS 2.1 entry points of 1Eh
          bytes, broken XSDTs, I/O APICs that read all ones or share GSIs,
          processors whose APIC IDs an IPI cannot name alone, VT-d units at
          address 0 or reading all ones, the interrupt remapping erratum of
          Intel's 5500, 5520, and X58 I/O hubs, pre-2016 firmware's ECAM
          reservations, the DMAR table's x2APIC opt-out, the TSC-deadline
          erratum of older microcode, and the C1E timer erratum of AMD's
          family 10h.
        - Measure the TSC's rate against the HPET or the PM timer, and take
          the processor's nominal rate where it agrees, rather than timing it
          with the firmware's Stall() alone.
        - Leave alone GPUs that have no display attached, whose firmware can
          hang when set to a mode.
        - Give the OS the firmware's EDID only as Linux 7.3 would take it,
          else one of ReBIOS's own.
        - Flush the whole ASID for the INVLPGA of the OS's own hypervisors,
          as Linux 7.3's KVM does for INVLPGA's errata on Genoa and Turin.
        - Hand the legacy BIOS the SMBIOS table's length and address in full,
          whose upper bytes came from uninitialised memory.
        - Set up every processor's control registers, PAT, and MTRRs alike,
          whatever the firmware left on each: the others could not call the
          firmware's runtime services, which may use SSE, shut down at
          machine checks, and kept MTRRs unlike the boot processor's.
        - Start the processors one at a time, only those that the MADT has
          enabled, with INIT and SIPIs of their own rather than broadcast
          ones: firmware marks disabled the local APICs that hang the
          machine when written to.
        - Restore the firmware's LVT entries when leaving x2APIC mode, take
          each LINT pin's NMI from the MADT as ACPI ranks its structures,
          fence the writes to x2APIC registers, select I/O APIC registers
          with byte writes, leave the firmware's SMI entries alone, and clear
          the remote IRRs that unacknowledged interrupts left set.
        - Give PCI interrupt links that the firmware left disabled an IRQ, as
          Linux 7.3 does, and take the SCI as active low and level-triggered
          where the MADT does not say otherwise.
        - End a call to the firmware's runtime services that takes a page
          fault, and call them no more, rather than panicking, as Linux 7.3
          does.
        - Quiesce the invalidation queue that the firmware left on in each
          VT-d unit, stop each AMD-Vi unit's command buffer and logs before
          the unit, as their specifications ask, and add the I/O APICs that
          some laptops' IVRS tables leave out, as Linux 7.3 does.
        - Disconnect the firmware's graphics output rather than its graphics
          driver, which left some panels dark.
        - Probe serial ports by their divisor latch, turn on their FIFOs, and
          take the UART's clock from the SPCR.
        - Allocate no memory after the first ExitBootServices() call, as UEFI
          requires, align boot sector reads as the drive asks, and clear the
          firmware's CPUID limit before anything reads CPUID.
        - Work around AMD's processor errata as Linux 7.3 does: its chicken
          bits, a cleared divider before every entry to the OS on Zen 1, no
          virtualised VMLOAD and VMSAVE on the Zen 4 models where they reset
          the machine, and a #UD in the OS rather than a panic for an INVLPGB
          that the processor refuses.
        - Turn on the decoding of a PCI serial card that ReBIOS logs to and of
          the card whose VBIOS runs, which firmware without drivers for them
          leaves off.
        - Drop the OS's translations on INIT and when it turns paging on, on
          Intel's processors, which kept Windows 10 from starting its other
          processors.
        - Emulate the PC's interrupt controllers as Linux 7.3's KVM does: keep
          the 8259s' in-service bits across ICW1, and pass their interrupts to
          local APIC LINT0 entries that ask for an NMI, as old Linux's NMI
          watchdog has them; clear an I/O APIC entry's Remote IRR when it is
          made edge-triggered; and in the local APICs, deliver nothing to one
          that is globally disabled, give a lowest priority interrupt to one
          that takes interrupts, take every INIT but the level de-assert,
          raise #GP for reserved bits of x2APIC mode's ICR and SELF IPI, count
          a late periodic timer's next period, and match physical destinations
          by the whole APIC ID.
        - Release the 8254's latched count when the counter is reprogrammed,
          as its datasheet has it.

    Miscellaneous:
        - Share the definitions and helpers that files repeated, and include
          only the headers that each file uses.
        - `make run` runs Nephrite, the fork of QEMU that ReBIOS is tested
          on, in place of QEMU, and emulates an Intel processor with VMX
          behind VT-d, or with `QEMU_VIRT=svm` an AMD one with SVM behind
          AMD-Vi, rather than nesting KVM.

2026-11-07  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.6.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.5.2:

    New features:
        - The OS can run hypervisors of its own, such as Linux's KVM, on AMD
          processors too: ReBIOS gives it the processor's SVM, with nested
          paging, NRIP saving, VMCB clean bits, flush by ASID, PAUSE
          filtering, LBR virtualisation, read-only guest page tables, page
          modification logging, TlbiCtl, decode assists, NMI virtualisation,
          the virtual GIF, virtual VMLOAD and VMSAVE, the guest mode execute
          trap, the TSC ratio MSR, SVM-Lock, the idle HLT intercept, the bus
          lock threshold, and FRED virtualisation, each where the processor
          has it, and with the guest VMCB address check.
        - On AMD processors, run the VMLOAD, VMSAVE, CLGI, and STGI of the
          OS's own hypervisors on the processor where it virtualises them,
          and have HLT exit to ReBIOS only where no interrupt waits for the
          OS, which saves exits.
        - The OS always gets the processor's VMX on Intel processors: the
          `nested` setting is obsolete, and ignored with a warning. ReBIOS
          sets aside 1/32 of the memory, at most 128 MiB, for the page tables
          it runs the guests of the OS's own hypervisors with.
        - Give the OS FRED, user interrupts, and, on Intel processors, APX,
          where the processor has them.
        - Give the OS INVLPGB and TLBSYNC, PCID, and the extended APIC space
          with its extended interrupt LVTs, on AMD processors that have them.
        - Post the OS's MSIs to its processors through the IOMMU where it can:
          with VT-d's posted interrupts, with IPI virtualisation or without,
          and with AMD-Vi's guest virtual APIC mode where AVIC runs.
        - On machines with several PCI host bridges or segments, show the OS
          one host bridge, with the other root buses behind ports on its bus
          0, so that OSes that know only one still find every device. The
          legacy BIOS follows the firmware's boot order for drives on those
          buses too, and _OSC grants the OS only what every host bridge does.
        - Make the OS's MCFG, MADT, and HPET tables describe the machine it
          gets rather than adjust the firmware's: one PCI segment, only the
          processors and I/O APICs it has, and no hidden HPET.
        - Keep the OS's port 92h in ReBIOS: its fast reset bit now sends INIT
          to the OS's processors, as the chipset's INIT# does, rather than
          reaching the machine's own.
        - On AMD processors with HOST_MCE_OVERRIDE, log the banks of a
          machine check that shuts down an OS without CR4.MCE.

    Bug fixes:
        - Keep the machine's page 0 out of the OS's memory: the OS's own
          hypervisors failed where it held their data structures, which
          ReBIOS took for missing.
        - Raise #UD, as the processor does, for the SVM instructions that an
          OS runs outside ring 0 without enabling SVM, rather than #GP.
        - Keep AMD's extended APIC registers when ReBIOS's own local APIC
          leaves x2APIC mode, so that its APIC IDs stay 8 bits wide.
        - Update Tendril, which now waits for USB devices to show up after an
          xHCI controller's reset.
        - Update SeaBIOS, which now reads a disc swapped into a CD drive
          rather than fail every read of it.

    Miscellaneous:
        - Align the code with the updated style rules.
        - Panic on processors that are neither Intel's nor AMD's, and on the
          other processor vendor's IOMMU, which only virtual machines have.

2026-11-03  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.5.2 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.5.1:

    New features:
        - All of ReBIOS, SeaBIOS included, now builds with Clang, LLD, and
          LLVM's tools with `TOOLCHAIN=llvm`, and with gold: a `-fuse-ld=`
          option in `LDFLAGS` now picks SeaBIOS's linker too.
        - The emulated VGA's video BIOS is now a small 8086 program in a 13 KiB
          ROM, down from 32 KiB, with the rest in the hypervisor instead of in
          16-bit C. The usual text calls run in the ROM, and in true real mode
          everything else takes a single VM exit, so both are much faster.
          Under V86, some calls, such as AX=4F01h, now take more VM exits than
          before.

    Bug fixes:
        - Give Windows x64, which runs a video BIOS in an x86 emulator, the
          right VBE modes: the old video BIOS listed them twice there, and with
          Clang not at all, which kept an installed Windows 7 at 16 colours.
        - Put the emulated VGA's linear framebuffer in the memory of the bridge
          above the VGA's card: an installed Windows 7 stayed at 16 colours on
          a card behind a bridge, as AMD's integrated graphics are.
        - Keep Windows Vista from crashing at boot (STOP 0x7E) when a driver
          gives a forwarded ACPI method an empty string, which ReBIOS's ACPI
          tables stored in their mailbox.
        - Date the BIOS 12/31/99, so that Windows 98 SE's Setup installs ACPI
          by itself: with an earlier date it did so only with `/p j`, and else
          found no PCI bus.
        - Add INT 10h AH=1Ch, which the video BIOS's functionality table
          already claimed.

    Miscellaneous:
        - The `SEABIOS_OBJDUMP` and `VGABIOS_*` make variables are gone, as
          nothing uses them any more.
        - `make clean` no longer needs a compiler for SeaBIOS.
        - Update SeaBIOS for building with Clang, LLD, and gold, and for the
          BIOS date, with a `CONFIG_BIOS_DATE` option. CI checks the gold
          builds too.

2026-11-02  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.5.1 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.5.0:

    New features:
        - `vga_emulation = never` now runs a video BIOS wherever there is one,
          even on graphics whose video BIOS ReBIOS would skip by default.

    Bug fixes:
        - Decide by lists of PCI IDs whose video BIOS to run by default, as
          those of recent integrated graphics can hang the machine. Cards in
          slots, now including AMD's RDNA ones, and graphics in the processor
          or soldered to the board that came out before 2018 run theirs. Those
          from 2018 on, now including AMD's Vega ones, get the emulated VGA.
          Graphics on neither list count as a card in a slot only if the
          machine has no battery, neither its ACPI power profile nor its SMBIOS
          chassis type rules slots out, and a PCI Express port above them has a
          slot.
        - Power off through ACPI's S5 state before the firmware's service, as
          Linux 7.3 does and as restarts already did: the service of a Dell
          Inspiron 7700 crashes by then.
        - Keep small prefetchable BARs in their bridge's prefetchable window,
          as firmware does, wherever that window is below 4 GiB, instead of in
          its memory window.
        - Where VM exits are slow, as under another hypervisor, hold the next
          timer tick through the 8259 until the OS has had as long as the last
          one took, for the 8254's ticks and for those of the HPET's legacy
          replacement route. A tick could otherwise come before what the last
          one interrupted ran again, over and over, which stalled Linux's boot.
          Elsewhere, ticks follow the counter alone.

2026-10-31  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.5.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.4.1:

    New features:
        - Emulate the 8254 timer on every machine, so that the OS and the
          legacy BIOS have a working one whether or not the chipset's runs. The
          chipset's now only sounds the speaker.
        - Emulate the 8042 keyboard controller on every machine. PS/2 devices
          plugged into a real 8042 keep working through it, and USB keyboards
          and mice reach the OS and the legacy BIOS as PS/2 ones. The ACPI
          tables show its devices where the firmware does not.
        - Drive xHCI, EHCI, OHCI, and UHCI controllers with the Tendril library
          until the OS takes them, with the handoff a BIOS's legacy USB support
          has, so that an OS without a USB driver keeps its keyboard and mouse.
        - Give the legacy BIOS the USB disks through the hypervisor, for
          booting from them and for INT 13h, in place of SeaBIOS's own USB
          stack.
        - New `legacy_usb` setting, `true` by default, for driving the USB
          controllers: with `false` they are left to the OS, and the legacy
          BIOS has no USB.
        - New `avic` setting for when AMD's AVIC runs the OS's local APICs:
          `auto`, the default, on Zen 4 and later processors with x2AVIC;
          `always`, on every processor that has it but those AMD advises
          against it on; or `never`.

    Bug fixes:
        - Restart through ACPI's reset register, the keyboard controller, or
          the reset control register before the firmware's reset service, as
          Linux 7.3 does: the service of a Dell Inspiron 7700 crashes by then.
        - Use AVIC by default only on Zen 4 and later processors with x2AVIC,
          as Linux 7.3's KVM does: Windows hung at its logo with AVIC on a
          Zen 3+ processor without x2AVIC, such as the Ryzen 7 7735HS.
        - Deliver an interrupt already on its way when the OS masks its source
          on the vector the OS programmed, not on one of ReBIOS's own, which
          the OS has no handler for.
        - Hold the OS up less while the emulated VGA redraws: a redraw runs a
          millisecond at a time between the OS's own work, takes at most a
          quarter of the boot processor's time, and, in VBE modes, looks only
          at the lines on pages the OS wrote to.
        - Hold the OS up less while the firmware's AML runs, for an ACPI call
          of the OS's or for a firmware event: it too runs a millisecond at a
          time between the OS's own work.

    Miscellaneous:
        - The OS's local APIC timers are kept in software, as the processor's
          own timer is now the hypervisor's.
        - The log reports VM exits that keep a processor from the OS for more
          than 5 ms.
        - The SeaBIOS banner shows the ReBIOS version, taken from Git or, in
          release tarballs, from a `.version` file.
        - Follow the latest PicoEFI C template, and update the
          freestanding-c-hdrs subproject.
        - `make run` gives QEMU a USB keyboard and mouse on xHCI, as the
          template does.
        - Update SeaBIOS to its latest upstream revision, without its USB
          stack, and add the Tendril subproject.

2026-10-27  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.4.1 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.4.0:

    New features:
        - Panels mounted sideways in their casing: ReBIOS shows its log and the
          emulated VGA's picture upright on the machines whose panel Linux
          knows to be mounted so, such as the Lenovo IdeaPad D330-10IGM and the
          Steam Deck, and the emulated video BIOS offers VBE modes and an EDID
          of the upright screen's size.

    Bug fixes:
        - Correct the framebuffer's size, which some firmware gives as
          landscape on portrait panels, as Linux does: the Lenovo IdeaPad
          D330-10IGM and IdeaPad Duet 3 10IGL5, and the Steam Deck. ReBIOS's
          log was garbled on them.
        - Emulate the VGA by default on panels mounted sideways, rather than
          run the graphics card's video BIOS, which draws sideways and turned
          the Lenovo IdeaPad D330-10IGM's screen off.
        - In PIC mode, keep PCI devices' interrupts off IRQs 6 and 14, which
          the legacy BIOS unmasks for its floppy and ATA drivers on any
          machine: a PCI device's level-triggered interrupt there flooded it,
          and it got no further than its "Machine UUID" line.
        - Ungate the 8254 timer on Apollo Lake, Gemini Lake, Elkhart Lake,
          Jasper Lake, and Alder Lake-N chipsets too, whose firmware can switch
          its clock off: the legacy BIOS then waited forever for its timer
          interrupt after its "Machine UUID" line.

2026-10-26  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.4.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.3.0:

    New features:
        - Intel integrated graphics from Skylake on: the OS gets the GPU's
          stolen memory at an address in its own memory map, which the GPU's
          registers then report, and the firmware's graphics page table entries
          move with it, as the IOMMU translates what the GPU accesses. The OS's
          memory no longer has a hole where the firmware put the stolen memory.
          Setting the new `igpu_stolen_remap` to `false` keeps the stolen
          memory at the firmware's address, as before, should the screen go
          black once the OS starts.
        - New `cpuid_limit` setting, which starts the OS with CPUID reporting
          no basic leaf past 2, as firmware setups' "Limit CPUID Maxval" option
          does, for OSes that fail on processors with more, such as Windows NT
          4.0.
        - Panics show more of the processor's state: its APIC ID, where in
          ReBIOS it stopped, the control registers, DR6 and DR7, the
          general-purpose registers, the top of the stack, and the OS's last VM
          exit.
        - With `verbose` on, ReBIOS shows its log on the screen for 30 seconds
          before it resets the machine because the OS triple faulted or got
          into a state that the processor cannot run it in.

    Bug fixes:
        - Take the legacy video BIOS only from the graphics card that the
          firmware displays on, or from the one the `vga` setting names, and
          emulate the VGA if that card has none, rather than run another
          card's.
        - Keep the firmware's graphics driver running while emulating the VGA:
          stopping it turned some screens off, such as a 2019 iMac's.
        - Emulate the VGA by default under Apple's firmware, rather than run
          the graphics card's video BIOS, which nothing vouches for on Macs: a
          2019 iMac's screen went dark with it.
        - Intel integrated graphics: give the OS the OpRegion, with the video
          BIOS table that describes the GPU's outputs, at an address in its own
          memory map, where the GPU's ASLS register now points. ASLS pointed at
          the firmware's address, which in the OS's memory map is other memory,
          so that graphics drivers found no video BIOS table (new in 0.2.0).
        - Let the OS reach BARs smaller than a page, such as the 2 KiB ones of
          some AHCI controllers, wherever it moves them. They could read as all
          ones and ignore writes.
        - Take the USB controllers from the firmware before the legacy BIOS
          drives them, as Linux does at boot, xHCI ones included, and drop the
          IOMMU memory reservations that only the firmware's USB code needed.
        - Give the OS a TSC of its own, offset from the processor's, rather
          than let it write the processor's, which ReBIOS keeps time with:
          Windows XP zeroes it at boot. IA32_TSC_ADJUST and TSC deadlines
          follow the OS's TSC.
        - Intel: write IA32_MCG_CTL to the processor only as all 0s or all 1s,
          the values Intel defines, and give the OS back the value it wrote,
          such as Windows NT 4.0's 0xffffffff.
        - Intel: keep frame buffer compression off on Skylake and Broxton
          integrated graphics, as Linux does where VT-d translates the GPU's
          accesses, which it always does under ReBIOS.

    Miscellaneous:
        - Refuse to start, with a notice, as without an IOMMU, where the
          integrated graphics malfunctions under VT-d, as Linux knows it to:
          Intel's Broadwell, and Ironlake in Arrandale processors, or in
          Clarkdale ones whose firmware sets up no shadow GTT.
        - The emulated VGA redraws only the scan lines the OS wrote to, rather
          than whole screens.
        - Follow the latest PicoEFI C template, and update the PicoEFI,
          freestanding-c-hdrs, and freestanding-lgrt subprojects.

2026-10-20  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.3.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.2.0:

    New features:
        - Nested virtualisation on Intel processors: with the new `nested`
          setting on, the OS can use VMX itself to run virtual machines of its
          own, e.g. with Linux's KVM, with EPT, VPID, VMCS shadowing, APIC
          virtualisation and posted interrupts, the VMX-preemption timer,
          page-modification logging, TSC scaling, EPTP switching, and the other
          VMX features the processor has. It stays off by default until AMD's
          SVM is nested as well.
        - Let the processor's local APIC virtualisation run the OS's local
          APICs: on Intel, TPR shadowing, APIC-access and APIC-register
          virtualisation, virtual-interrupt delivery, posted interrupts, x2APIC
          virtualisation, and IPI virtualisation; on AMD, AVIC and x2AVIC.
          Local APIC accesses, such as 32-bit Windows' TPR writes, rarely stop
          the OS anymore: Windows 10 installs in half the time it took under
          nested KVM.
        - AMD: use flush by ASID, VMCB clean bits, decode assists, and virtual
          NMIs and GIF where the processor has them.
        - Interrupts that the OS programs into devices (MSIs and MSI-X entries)
          and into the I/O APICs arrive on vectors that ReBIOS hands out, which
          it then delivers as the OS asked: whatever the OS writes, no device
          interrupts ReBIOS's own vectors, and the NMIs and INITs the OS asks
          for reach its own processors only. Where the IOMMUs can, VT-d or
          AMD-Vi interrupt remapping carries these interrupts and blocks the
          ones devices send otherwise, e.g. by DMA.
        - Pass the processor's thermal and performance monitoring interrupts,
          and Intel's hardware feedback table, to the OS, so that its scheduler
          gets the processor's hints (Intel Thread Director).
        - Let the OS use Intel PT where its output can go through EPT, PEBS
          where its records can, MPX, LAM, and AMD's ERAPS, Upper Address
          Ignore, and LBR virtualisation.
        - Show ReBIOS's log, and any panic, on the screen with Flanterm.

    Bug fixes:
        - Map the framebuffer wherever the firmware put it: where that was
          above 4 GiB, as for many dedicated graphics cards, the emulated VGA
          stopped ReBIOS before showing anything (new in 0.2.0).
        - Bounce the BIOS's NVMe transfers to buffers that are not dword
          aligned, which the controller cannot take. FreeDOS did not boot from
          NVMe drives.
        - Emulated VGA: put the linear framebuffer in PCI memory that no device
          uses rather than in the BAR of a PCI function of ReBIOS's own.
          Windows 8 and later saw two display adapters, drawing both into the
          same screen.
        - Carry out hardware task switches as the processor does: their steps
          in its order, privilege checks for the new task's data segments,
          double faults for faults while an exception's switch runs, the stack
          limit for its error code, the EXT bit, the T flag's debug trap, and
          the reserved EFLAGS bits.
        - Walk the OS's paging as the processor does where ReBIOS accesses its
          memory: accessed and dirty flags, reserved bits, SMEP, SMAP,
          protection keys, and LASS.
        - Decode 32-bit real mode code (CS.D set in real mode) as 32-bit code,
          and check every iteration of the string instructions ReBIOS carries
          out against its segment's limit and rights.
        - Wrap RIP at 4 GiB outside 64-bit mode after the instructions ReBIOS
          carries out.
        - Debuggers work across the accesses ReBIOS carries out: data and I/O
          breakpoints on emulated memory and ports, RF as the processor keeps
          it, branch single-stepping (BTF), and the effects of debug traps'
          delivery on DR7 and DEBUGCTL.
        - Raise #GP for the control register writes the processor refuses, such
          as paging without protected mode, rather than reset, and flush the
          OS's TLB after the ones ReBIOS carries out.
        - Hand the OS what ReBIOS does not expect as the processor would,
          rather than stop: unknown instructions' exits as #UD, states that
          cannot be entered as a triple fault, code in emulated memory, and
          interrupt delivery onto a stack in it, as in VGA memory.
        - Keep the CPUID limit and the XD disable bit of IA32_MISC_ENABLE to
          the OS, which could otherwise limit ReBIOS's own CPUID or break its
          VM exits.
        - AMD: keep the OS's memory out of the HyperTransport address range,
          where the IOMMU and the processor give addresses special meanings.
        - AMD: give the OS a memory configuration of its own (SYSCFG, TOP_MEM,
          TOP_MEM2, IORRs, MMIO_CFG_BASE), keep the TSC's rate whatever its
          P-state definitions say, and load none of its microcode.
        - Intel: set the XSS-exiting bitmap where XSAVES is enabled.
        - Keep device interrupts off APIC ID FFh, the broadcast.
        - Give the OS memory with no regard to where the firmware's own memory
          types were, so that it has no holes there.

    Miscellaneous:
        - The OS gets only the CPU features, MSRs, and XSAVE components that
          ReBIOS knows, the model-specific MSRs by the processor model's or
          family's tables in Intel's and AMD's manuals. Those it does not
          support yet stay hidden, such as FRED, user interrupts, SGX, and
          memory encryption.
        - Hide the HPET from the OS where its timers can deliver interrupts
          through the FSB, as messages that would go wherever the OS pointed
          them.
        - Performance counters count only the OS's own work.
        - The profile names the exits of VMX instructions.
        - Cite the newest revisions of the documents and projects that the code
          follows.

2026-10-18  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.2.0 ***

    Noteworthy changes compared to the previous release, 0.1.0:

    New features:
        - The OS now gets ACPI tables that ReBIOS generates, in ACPI 1.0 AML
          that even Windows 98 understands, rather than copies of the
          firmware's. Their methods call into ReBIOS, which runs the firmware's
          AML itself, and so the OS still sees the machine's devices and their
          resources, gets their events and notifications, the embedded
          controller's included, and powers off. Windows 98 installs and runs
          in ACPI mode, and the memory the firmware keeps for its AML no longer
          breaks up the OS's.
        - The ReBIOS USB stick: `make usb` builds an image for a USB stick that
          runs ReBIOS and boots the computer's other drives, which then need no
          FAT partition for ReBIOS. Releases ship it as `rebios-usb.img`.
        - New `boot_drive` setting, for the drive the legacy BIOS boots first:
          the one ReBIOS was started from (`self`, the default), one ReBIOS
          picks (`auto`: drives with something to boot first, internal ones,
          then CDs and DVDs, then external ones), or one named by its UEFI
          device path or by part of its model name.
        - Lay out PCI memory anew for the OS, without moving BARs on the
          hardware: BARs of 256 MiB or less come below 4 GiB, larger ones stay
          above it for 64-bit OSes, and the OS's memory below 4 GiB reaches up
          to those BARs, even where the firmware ends low memory well below
          them. The OS gets MTRRs that match.
        - Emulate the A20 gate of port 92h and of the keyboard controller:
          while the OS keeps it closed, every odd MiB of memory mirrors the
          even one below it, as on a PC.
        - New `profile` setting, which logs every 10 seconds how often and why
          the OS makes ReBIOS step in. A debugging aid.

    Bug fixes:
        - Carry out the OS's hardware task switches on Intel processors, which
          leave them to the hypervisor. ReBIOS used to stop.
        - Let addresses with nothing behind them read as all ones, and drop
          writes to them, as on a PC. ReBIOS used to stop.
        - Reserve, in the OS's memory map, the address space below 4 GiB that
          no memory backs, so that the OS places no device there.
        - Emulate string I/O, such as REP OUTSW, to the ports ReBIOS emulates.
          Windows 7's boot graphics on the emulated VGA stopped ReBIOS.
        - Emulated VGA: build the Video BIOS for the 386, so that the x86
          emulators that run Video BIOSes, such as 64-bit Windows', find no
          instructions they lack. Windows 7 x64 showed a black screen after its
          boot animation.
        - Emulated VGA: return the VBE mode list in the caller's information
          block, where 64-bit Windows' emulator finds it. Windows 7 x64 ran in
          16 colours.
        - Raise the OS's single-step traps after the instructions ReBIOS
          carries out for it, and after each iteration of a string instruction,
          as the processor would. Debuggers lost control stepping over trapped
          port I/O, CPUID, or emulated memory accesses.
        - Let pending interrupts in as soon as the OS has taken an exception
          that ReBIOS raised, rather than at its next unrelated exit.
        - Intel: block NMIs again for an IRET from an NMI handler that an EPT
          violation interrupted, as the SDM requires.
        - Intel: hide from CPUID the instructions VMX would not let the OS run:
          RDTSCP, RDPID, INVPCID, XSAVES, WAITPKG, PCONFIG, MSRLIST, and
          PBNDKB.
        - AMD Family 10h and 12h: work around erratum 383, as AMD's revision
          guide asks of hypervisors.
        - AMD Family 15h Models 10h-1Fh: work around IOMMU erratum 746, and
          wait for the IOMMU event log entries its tail pointer ran ahead of
          (erratum 732).
        - Intel: keep up the DMA translation the firmware left on for the
          integrated graphics of recent generations, which can hang when it is
          turned off, and map only small pages on Sandy Bridge's IOMMUs, which
          do not implement the large ones they claim.
        - Connect the storage controllers the firmware left alone, as with fast
          boot enabled, so that their drives can be ordered.
        - Allocate the loader's memory below 4 GiB where there is room, as some
          firmware leaves memory above 4 GiB out of its page tables.
        - Refuse memory maps whose descriptors are too small to read, and VT-d
          units with nothing answering at their registers, rather than misread
          them.

    Miscellaneous:
        - ReBIOS is now licensed under the GNU GPL version 2 only, rather than
          version 3 or later, so that GPLv2-only code, such as Linux's, can be
          used. SeaBIOS, under the GNU LGPL version 3, remains a separate
          program, which ReBIOS carries and runs as the legacy BIOS.
        - Emulated VGA: VBE modes in the screen's own size and format draw
          straight to the screen.
        - Cache VMCS reads and writes between VM entries, which speeds up
          ReBIOS when it runs nested, as under KVM.
        - `make run` runs QEMU with nested KVM and an Intel IOMMU.
        - Follow the latest PicoEFI C template: relocations are packed where
          the linker can, and builds cope with distribution flags that add
          `-fexceptions` and with Clang drivers that ignore `-fuse-ld`.
        - Update uACPI to its latest revision, beyond 7.0.0, and the PicoEFI,
          freestanding-c-hdrs, and freestanding-lgrt (formerly cc-runtime)
          subprojects.
        - Releases come with this ChangeLog, which their notes link to.

2026-10-16  Mintsuki  <mintsuki@protonmail.com>

    *** Release 0.1.0 ***

    The first release.
